// What `playdeck.video` is: a Cloudflare Worker that serves static assets and
// nothing else. `.github/workflows/deploy-site.yml` builds `apps/site`,
// assembles it into the directory named below and hands this file to Wrangler
// (#519). That build is the whole of what is served — the Storybook workbench
// was published under `storybook/` until #534 and is a local development tool
// now.
//
// At the repository root rather than in `apps/site`, because what it publishes
// is an assembled directory rather than a build output: `deploy-dist/` is
// written by `scripts/assemble-deploy.mjs` and belongs to the deployment, not
// to the app whose `dist/` currently fills it.
{
  "name": "playdeck",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-29",

  // No `main`. Cloudflare's documentation makes the key optional for an
  // assets-only Worker, and there is no Worker script here to point it at:
  // every request this deployment answers is a file on disk. `assets.binding`
  // is absent for the same reason — a binding exists so a Worker script can
  // fetch an asset, and adding one would declare a handler that does not exist.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./deploy-dist/",

    // `"none"` — Cloudflare's default, restated because the alternatives are
    // both wrong here rather than merely unchosen. `"404-page"` would make this
    // file depend on a document the site is under no obligation to emit,
    // and the dependency would break silently: with no such document the
    // request falls back to Cloudflare's own 404, which is what `"none"` gives
    // already, so nothing ever reports that the setting found nothing to serve.
    // `"single-page-application"` would be worse than absent: it rewrites every
    // miss to the root `index.html` and answers it 200, so a mistyped URL would
    // render the landing page and report success. The site is not a
    // single-page application in the sense that setting means — it is a static
    // build with a real document at every route it has.
    "not_found_handling": "none"

    // `html_handling` is left at its default, `auto-trailing-slash`, and the
    // default is still load-bearing now that the site is the only thing served.
    // Astro's default build format writes a route as a directory with an
    // `index.html` inside it — `reference/index.html`,
    // `guides/contract/index.html` — and `apps/site/astro.config.ts` sets
    // neither `build.format` nor `trailingSlash`, so every route in the
    // artifact has that shape. The default serves `/reference/` from the index
    // inside it and redirects `/reference` onto it, which is the pair of
    // behaviours a directory-format build needs and the only pair it needs.
    //
    // Each alternative gives up part of that. `"none"` rewrites nothing, so a
    // request for a directory finds no file and every route but the root 404s
    // unless the build is switched to extensionless documents. `"drop"`
    // redirects away from the form the site itself emits — every internal link
    // in the built pages carries the slash, because Astro writes them from the
    // same directory-format routes. `"force"` redirects towards it, which
    // agrees with the build, and is still the narrower of the two: it decides
    // on the shape of the URL where the default decides on what the artifact
    // holds.
  },

  // Cloudflare creates the DNS record and the certificate for a custom domain
  // itself, which is why an apex works here at all — nothing in this repository
  // ever writes a CNAME, so the rule that a zone apex cannot carry one never
  // comes up. Two conditions have to hold for the route to attach: the zone is
  // active in Cloudflare, which means the domain's nameservers point at
  // Cloudflare rather than at the registrar's; and the hostname carries no
  // existing CNAME record, which Cloudflare refuses to take a custom domain
  // over. A deploy that runs before both hold uploads the assets and fails to
  // bind the hostname.
  "routes": [{ "pattern": "playdeck.video", "custom_domain": true }]
}
